From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:49:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] chore(cms): add migration script to fix the 2 Projects
 documents
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Checked the live data directly (GROQ, via browser — direct Node fetch
to api.sanity.io is blocked in this sandbox, same as earlier this
session): the 2 existing `project` documents in Sanity
(project-capacity-building, project-psychotraumatology) were created
against an older, different schema shape — plain-string title,
description, slug, startDate/endDate, status, tags, a portable-text
body — none of which studio/schemas/project.ts currently reads (order,
icon, title/period as localizedString, desc as localizedText,
highlights as an array of localizedText).

This is the exact same "schema redefined without a data migration"
issue diagnosed and fixed for `department` documents earlier this
session (migrate-department-cards.mjs) — Sanity never deletes data
when a field is renamed, Studio just can't display it under the new
field names, so both documents show as "Untitled project", and the
homepage's useProjects() hook finds no usable title and silently falls
back to the hardcoded PROJECTS array in
src/app/data/departments.ts — which is why nobody had noticed.

scripts/migrate-project-cards.mjs sets the new fields (title, period,
desc, highlights, icon, order) from the same trilingual copy that
hardcoded fallback already uses (src/locales/{en,fr,rw}/home.json ->
projects.items), so Studio ends up showing exactly what the site
already renders. Only adds the new fields — the legacy
description/slug/startDate/endDate/status/tags/body fields are left
untouched. Dry-run by default, --apply to write, skips any document
that already has title.en set so it's safe to re-run.

Verified the field-mapping logic offline against the real locale data
(network to Sanity is blocked in this sandbox, so the live dry-run
itself needs to be run locally) — both documents' title/period/desc/
4 highlights each resolve correctly in all three languages.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
